Phosphor-Bop
Ingredients: FREE JAZZ / AVANT-GARDE + BEBOP + HYPNOTIC, TRANCE-LIKE & MINIMAL + Tenor Saxophone  
Creative Catalyst: The scene bloomed in a water-damaged ghost mall where latchkey runaways rewired discarded arcade cabinets into microtonal drone sequencers.

Phosphor-Bop sounds like John Coltrane's ghost trapped inside an overheating, overclocked Galaga soundboard that refuses to reboot: blisteringly fast 300-BPM bebop changes and feral, overblown saxophone multiphonics pinned against relentless, unyielding minimalist repetition, humming with the high-voltage electromagnetic squeal of dying CRT monitors.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 260–320 BPM. Blistering hard-bop swing subdivisions mathematically forced into frozen, motorik 4/4 and 11/8 trance pulses; kick drums are replaced by heavy microswitches and arcade coin returns clattering in dizzying, machine-gun cycles.
- Harmonic Palette: Chromatic Coltrane changes (augmented major thirds, sharp ninths) hammered into rigid, modal drone centers (D Dorian, Phrygian dominant), corroded by the microtonal detuning of decaying Yamaha FM synthesizer soundchips.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Flyback Coupling: Shoving a vintage dynamic microphone directly into the unshielded high-voltage flyback transformer of a CRT arcade monitor to generate an abrasive, screaming 15.7 kHz electromagnetic halo around the tenor saxophone.
  2. Token-Gated Chopping: Master buses hard-sliced through physical arcade microswitches pressed at inhuman tempos, producing rhythmic glitch stutters.
  3. Dead-Mall Reverberation: Cavernous, natural acoustic delay created by placing horn players in subterranean mall service corridors while re-amping the audio through abandoned department store intercom horns.
- Dynamics: An endurance-testing, flatline intensity that builds not through quiet-to-loud volume swells, but through dizzying timbral saturation—layering cyclical saxophone permutations until listeners experience auditory phantom melodies amidst the noise.

Scene & Origin Lore
The movement ignited in the subterranean ruins of the St. Jude Galleria, a dead two-million-square-foot mall marooned on an exurban highway belt. Squatting teenagers dragged gas generators into the shuttered "Tokens & Quests" arcade, tearing open cabinet backs to solder patch cables across Yamaha YM2151 soundchips and arcade coin acceptors, transforming defunct gaming rigs into screaming polyrhythmic step-sequencers. Subculture devotees dress in grease-stained surplus mail-carrier parkas, deadstock oversized polo shirts cinched with computer ribbon cables, and fingerless mechanic gloves worn to survive hours of joystick-slapping. Their defining ritual is "The Kill-Screen Vigil": an ensemble locks into an uninterrupted, four-hour bebop sprint against an arcade board glitching into memory overflow, horn players cycling through circular breathing while the audience presses their foreheads against buzzing CRT glass to absorb the sub-harmonic bone conduction until the transformer blows.

Typical Instruments
- Tenor Saxophone: The undisputed lead voice; heavily battered vintage horns equipped with neck contact mics, overblown into screeching altissimo harmonics and circular-breathed bebop arpeggios.
- The Cabinet-Harp: A gutted Street Fighter II housing rewired with piano wire, piezo pickups, and optical coin-slot triggers to produce relentless industrial ostinatos.
- Circuit-Bent FM Synth Chips (Yamaha YM2612 / YM2151): Salvaged arcade sound engines hardwired to output unstable square waves and microtonal chiptune drones.
- Upright Double Bass: Played percussively without a bow, using cracked plastic arcade tokens to saw against the strings near the bridge.
- Prepared Cyclical Drum Kits: Standard jazz kits modified with cracked crash cymbals screwed directly into metal arcade coin doors.

